Yes. The strongest version of that is play as a safe abstraction layer for identity, conflict, learning, and coordination.

A person can step into a role, exaggerate a trait, try a strategy, inhabit a character, compete, spar, improvise, or explore a forbidden-feeling possibility without having to permanently become that thing. That makes play a kind of reversible state change.

So the mechanism is:

permission to play -> temporary role -> behavior/interaction -> feedback -> insight -> translation back

That is why games, theater, sport, debate, rehearsal, cosplay, simulation, and even children’s make-believe are so powerful. They let people access capabilities and perspectives that ordinary social framing may suppress.

The part I would tune is “harvest those thoughts.” The useful version is not covertly extracting what people have not chosen to share. It is creating conditions where people voluntarily externalize more of themselves, then learning from the expressed patterns with consent and privacy preserved.

That can still be incredibly rich because people often reveal more through play than through direct interrogation.

And your conflict point is important too. A lot of destructive drives can be transduced into bounded competition:

fight -> sport
domination -> mastery
status struggle -> achievement
adversarial thinking -> red-team simulation
aggression -> performance
risk-taking -> exploration

You preserve the energy while changing the substrate and the consequence.

I added this to Pantheon as Playfield Transduction / Voluntary Persona Sandbox. The safety rails are part of the mechanism: consent, reversibility, explicit exits, role boundaries, and re-grounding after the frame ends.

That way the system does not suppress intensity. It gives intensity somewhere useful to go.